WEBVTT
TITLE Exact counts: comparisons, probes, transfers and multiplications — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Section 5 of the syllabus asks for "asymptotic worst case time and space complexity" — and the paper then asks for an integer.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Four units cover every count the syllabus asks for: a comparison, a probe, a block transfer, a multiplication.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Merge sort on eight elements does 24 units of work and at most 17 comparisons. Both are right; they answer different questions.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The procedure: name the unit, count the units per level, sum with the structure's identity, then check by a second route.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Merge sort on eight elements: three levels below the root, eight leaves, seven merges.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Work: every level moves all eight elements once, so the total is 8 times 3 = 24 units.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Comparisons: 12 best, 17 worst, 15.73 on average, over all 40,320 orderings of eight elements.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The closed form n times ceil(lg n) minus 2 to the ceil(lg n) plus 1 gives 17 at n = 8, and matches the enumeration at every n from 1 to 8.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A partition compares the pivot with every other element in its range: exactly n - 1 comparisons for n elements.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Worst case: every partition splits off one element, so the counts are 7 + 6 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 28 at n = 8.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Average: 16.92 at n = 8, matching 2(n+1)H(n) - 4n. Best case: 13.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
GATE CSE 2014 Set 1 asked for two inputs: {1,2,3,4,5} takes 10 comparisons and {4,1,5,3,2} takes 6, so t1 > t2.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Binary search makes one comparison per level, and the range halves each time, so there are floor(lg n) + 1 levels.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Checked against every size and every target up to 64: the worst case is always floor(lg n) + 1, and an unsuccessful search costs the same.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
At n = 1024 that is 11 comparisons: ten halvings reach one element, and the eleventh is the test on it.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
GATE CSE 2007 Q15 counts a doubling loop: floor(lg n) + 1 is the answer, because the exit test is not a comparison made while the loop runs.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Ten keys in seven chained slots: the load factor alpha is 10/7 = 1.43.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Successful search: 14 probes over 10 keys, an average of 1.4. Unsuccessful: 17 probes over 7 home slots, an average of 2.43.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The estimates 1 + alpha/2 and alpha are averages over random hash functions, not predictions for one table.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
For open addressing the estimate is 1/(1 - alpha), which is 2 at alpha = 0.5 and 10 at alpha = 0.9. GATE DS&AI 2024 Q11 asked exactly this.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Sixteen words in a four-by-four array, block size four words, a two-block cache. One block transfer per miss.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Row-major order: 4 misses and 12 hits, because each block is used four times before it is evicted.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Column-major order: 16 misses and no hits. Every consecutive access wants a different row, and the cache holds two.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Turn the count into a time: with a one-cycle hit and a 40-cycle miss penalty, average access time is 11 cycles row-major and 41 column-major.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Matrix-chain multiplication on five matrices: the subproblems are the contiguous ranges, and there are 5 times 4 / 2 = 10 of them.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Filling it costs one split test per possible break: 4 + 6 + 6 + 4 = 20 split points, which is n(n squared - 1)/6.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
A different shape, a different count: LCS on strings of length 4 and 5 fills (4+1)(5+1) = 30 cells.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The rule: cells are counted from the table's shape, transitions from what each cell reads. Neither is a complexity class.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Schoolbook multiplication of two n-digit numbers makes four half-size products, so at depth k there are 4 to the k scalar products.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Karatsuba reuses two products to get the middle term, so there are only 3 to the k: 243 against 1024 at k = 5.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The exponent is log base 2 of 3 = 1.585, and the model checks the identity 3 to the k equals n to the 1.585 at n = 1024.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
At n = 1024 the two counts are 59049 and 1048576 — a factor of about 18, from one changed call.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
One size, 1024 elements, and four questions. Binary search: 11 comparisons.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Merge sort: 10240 units of work, and at most 9217 comparisons in the worst case.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Quicksort worst case: 1024 times 1023 / 2 = 523776 comparisons. Cache: 1024 words in 256 blocks is 256 transfers.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Karatsuba: 59049 multiplications, against 1048576 for the schoolbook method. Four correct answers, one n.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step 1 and 2: name the unit, then count the units in one level, one partition, one call.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step 3 and 4: multiply by the structure's own identity, then reach the same integer a second way.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
The five traps: work for comparisons, the root counted as a level, lg n without its plus one, a rate used as a count, a table's average used as its answer.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Nothing here replaces the asymptotic class. The class tells you what grows; the count tells you what the paper is asking for.

